      "text": "Given that the mean and median of a set of three numbers are both 4, and the smallest number is 1, we can deduce the following:\n\n1. The set of numbers must have three elements: \\( \\{a, b, c\\} \\).\n2. The median of the set is the middle element, so \\( b = 4 \\).\n3. The mean of the set is the average of the three numbers, so \\( \\frac{a + b + c}{3} = 4 \\). Since \\( b = 4 \\), we have \\( \\frac{a + 4 + c}{3} = 4 \\), which simplifies to \\( a + 4 + c = 12 \\) or \\( a + c = 8 \\).\n4. We know \\( a = 1 \\) (the smallest number) and \\( c \\) must be greater than 4 to maintain the median at 4. The only value for \\( c \\) that satisfies \\( a + c = 8 \\) and \\( a = 1 \\) is \\( c = 7 \\).\n\nThus, the set of numbers is \\( \\{1, 4, 7\\} \\). The range of a set of numbers is the difference between the largest and smallest numbers, so the range is \\( 7 - 1 = 6 \\).\n\nLet's confirm this with a Python code implementation to ensure the reasoning is correct.\n```python\n# Given values\nmean = 4\nmedian = 4\nsmallest_number = 1\n\n# Since the median is 4, the middle element (b) in the set {a, b, c} is 4\nb = median\n\n# The mean is given by (a + b + c) / 3 = 4\n# Substituting b = 4, we get (a + 4 + c) / 3 = 4, which simplifies to a + c = 8\n# We also know that a = 1, so c = 8 - a\nc = 8 - smallest_number\n\n# The set of numbers is {1, 4, 7}\nnumbers = [smallest_number, b, c]\nrange_of_set = max(numbers) - min(numbers)\nprint(range_of_set)\n```\n```output\n6\n```\nThe range of the set of numbers \\(\\{1, 4, 7\\}\\) is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).",
