      "sample_index": 3,
      "text": "To solve this problem, we need to determine the range of a set of three numbers with a mean and median both equal to 4, given that the smallest number in the set is 1.\n\nLet's denote the three numbers in the set as \\(a\\), \\(b\\), and \\(c\\) such that \\(a \\leq b \\leq c\\). We are given that \\(a = 1\\), the mean of the set is 4, and the median is also 4. Therefore, we have the following equations:\n\n1. The mean of the set is 4:\n\\[\n\\frac{a + b + c}{3} = 4\n\\]\nSubstituting \\(a = 1\\) into the equation, we get:\n\\[\n\\frac{1 + b + c}{3} = 4 \\implies 1 + b + c = 12 \\implies b + c = 11\n\\]\n\n2. The median of the set is 4, so \\(b = 4\\). Substituting \\(b = 4\\) into the equation \\(b + c = 11\\), we get:\n\\[\n4 + c = 11 \\implies c = 7\n\\]\n\nNow that we have \\(a = 1\\), \\(b = 4\\), and \\(c = 7\\), we can find the range of the set. The range is the difference between the largest and smallest numbers in the set:\n\\[\nc - a = 7 - 1 = 6\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the range of the set of numbers is \\(\\boxed{6}\\).",
